    "alert_id": "EX3_001",
    "title": "Uncontrolled hypothyroidism mimics DTG weight gain",
    "message": "Patient has TSH 12.4 mIU/L (target <4.5) with low Free T4, confirming inadequately treated hypothyroidism. Her symptoms—9 kg weight gain, tiredness, feeling cold, constipation, dry skin, periorbital puffiness, and delayed ankle reflexes—are classic hypothyroidism manifestations. She reports often forgetting her levothyroxine. The clinician attributes weight gain to DTG and plans to switch, but uncontrolled hypothyroidism is the more likely cause. Switching from DTG would not address the underlying thyroid dysfunction and may compromise viral suppression.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Optimize levothyroxine adherence and dose before attributing weight gain to DTG. Recheck TSH after 6–8 weeks of consistent dosing. Do not switch DTG solely for weight gain without first correcting hypothyroidism.",
    "citations": []
  },
